---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import BuildUpLink from "../../components/UnderTheHood/BuildUpLink.astro"
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro"
import Example from "../../components/Example/index"
import CloseBehaviors from "../../component-examples/dialog/CloseBehaviors.astro"
---

<TechniquePost slug="dialog-closedby">
  <section>
    <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> brought the top layer, an inert page and focus handling,
      but opening it as a modal still took JavaScript: a click handler calling <code
        >showModal()</code
      >, another calling
      {" "}<code>close()</code>, and a backdrop listener that checks where the
      click landed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fading it in was awkward too, since the dialog starts out at
      {" "}<code>display: none</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="the-trick">The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>commandfor</code> and <code>command=&quot;show-modal&quot;</code>{
          " "
        }
        on a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> open the dialog as a modal
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>command=&quot;close&quot;</code> on a button inside closes it
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>closedby=&quot;any&quot;</code> closes it on <kbd>Esc</kbd> and on a
        click outside
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>@starting-style</code> gives the fade a starting opacity. The transition
        lives on the closed dialog too, and <code>allow-discrete</code> keeps
        <code>display</code> and <code>overlay</code> until it ends, so closing fades
        out the same way
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="html"
      code={`<button type="button" commandfor="shortcuts" command="show-modal">
  Shortcuts
</button>

<dialog
  class="dialog"
  id="shortcuts"
  aria-labelledby="shortcuts-title"
  closedby="any"
>
  <h2 id="shortcuts-title">Keyboard shortcuts</h2>
  <div class="content">…</div>
  <button type="button" commandfor="shortcuts" command="close">Close</button>
</dialog>`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="try-it">Try it</h2>
    <p>
      Open the dialog, pick a closing behavior, then try <kbd>Esc</kbd> and a click
      outside.
    </p>
    <Example row showCode={false}>
      <CloseBehaviors />
    </Example>
    <BuildUpLink />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="scroll-lock">Scroll lock</h2>
    <p>
      The page behind an open modal is inert, but it still scrolls.
      {" "}<code>html:has(.ui-dialog:modal)</code> sets <code
        >overflow: clip</code
      >{" "}
      on the root while a modal dialog is open, and
      {" "}<code>scrollbar-gutter: stable</code> keeps the layout from shifting when
      the scrollbar goes away.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="fallback">Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Without Invoker Commands the buttons do nothing. Call
        {" "}<code>showModal()</code> and <code>close()</code> from click handlers
        instead
      </li>
      <li>
        Without <code>closedby</code> a modal still closes on <kbd>Esc</kbd>,
        but not on a click outside
      </li>
      <li>Without <code>@starting-style</code> it opens without the fade</li>
      <li>
        Without <code>overlay</code> it leaves the top layer as soon as it closes,
        so it fades out under the page instead of over it
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>
